---
description: C#/.NET service conventions
alwaysApply: true
---

# Stack
- .NET (latest LTS), C#, nullable enabled, implicit usings.
- Messaging: Kafka via Confluent.Kafka. Producers/consumers live in
  Infrastructure/Messaging. Always use idempotent producer config.
- Storage: TimescaleDB (Postgres) via Npgsql/Dapper — no EF Core migrations
  unless a project already uses EF. Hypertables for time-series tables.
- Deploy target: Kubernetes (see /k8s manifests), observability via
  Prometheus/Grafana — expose /metrics on every service.

# Project layout
- Each service: <ServiceName>/{Api,Application,Domain,Infrastructure}
  + <ServiceName>.Tests (xUnit).
- Shared libs under /libs.

# Testing & verification loop (do this after every change)
1. `dotnet build` — must be warning-free before proceeding.
2. `dotnet test` — all unit tests must pass.
3. `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d --build`
4. Poll health endpoints (`/healthz`) and Kafka/Timescale readiness
   before declaring success — don't just check container "Up" status.
5. `docker compose logs --tail=200 <service>` — check for exceptions,
   not just exit code 0.
6. If anything fails, read the actual error, fix the root cause
   (not just silence the symptom), and repeat from step 1.
7. `docker compose down` when the loop finishes successfully.

# Style
- Use minimal APIs unless the existing project uses controllers.
- Async all the way down; no `.Result`/`.Wait()`.
- Structured logging (Serilog), no Console.WriteLine in service code.